## Solution

**Answer-leak check:** The problem text contains no leaked answers, solution sketches, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

**Setup.** We seek $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ (with $\mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$) satisfying
$$f(f(n))+f(n+1)=n+2 \quad\text{for all } n\ge 1. \tag{$\star$}$$

**Determining $f(1)$.** Setting $n=1$ in $(\star)$: $f(f(1))+f(2)=3$. Since $f$ maps to positive integers, the only possibilities are $(f(f(1)),\,f(2))\in\{(1,2),(2,1)\}$.

*Case A:* $f(1)=1$, giving $f(f(1))=f(1)=1$ and $f(2)=2$. ✓ (consistent)

*Case B:* $f(f(1))=2$, $f(2)=1$. Let $f(1)=a$, so $f(a)=2$. Since $f(2)=1\ne 2$, we need $a\ge 3$. From $n=2$: $f(f(2))+f(3)=f(1)+f(3)=a+f(3)=4$, so $f(3)=4-a\ge 1$, forcing $a\le 3$, i.e., $a=3$. But then $f(a)=f(3)=4-3=1\ne 2$, contradicting $f(a)=2$. ✗

So **$f(1)=1$ is forced**, and the recurrence uniquely determines every subsequent value (since one verifies inductively that $f(n)\le n$, so $f(f(n))$ is always already known when computing $f(n+1)$).

**Computing $f(1),\dots,f(20)$.** Using $f(f(n))+f(n+1)=n+2$, i.e., $f(n+1)=n+2-f(f(n))$:

| $n$ | $f(n)$ | $f(f(n))$ | $f(n+1)=n+2-f(f(n))$ |
|-----|--------|-----------|---------------------|
| 1 | 1 | $f(1)=1$ | $3-1=2$ |
| 2 | 2 | $f(2)=2$ | $4-2=2$ |
| 3 | 2 | $f(2)=2$ | $5-2=3$ |
| 4 | 3 | $f(3)=2$ | $6-2=4$ |
| 5 | 4 | $f(4)=3$ | $7-3=4$ |
| 6 | 4 | $f(4)=3$ | $8-3=5$ |
| 7 | 5 | $f(5)=4$ | $9-4=5$ |
| 8 | 5 | $f(5)=4$ | $10-4=6$ |
| 9 | 6 | $f(6)=4$ | $11-4=7$ |
| 10 | 7 | $f(7)=5$ | $12-5=7$ |
| 11 | 7 | $f(7)=5$ | $13-5=8$ |
| 12 | 8 | $f(8)=5$ | $14-5=9$ |
| 13 | 9 | $f(9)=6$ | $15-6=9$ |
| 14 | 9 | $f(9)=6$ | $16-6=10$ |
| 15 | 10 | $f(10)=7$ | $17-7=10$ |
| 16 | 10 | $f(10)=7$ | $18-7=11$ |
| 17 | 11 | $f(11)=7$ | $19-7=12$ |
| 18 | 12 | $f(12)=8$ | $20-8=12$ |
| 19 | 12 | $f(12)=8$ | $21-8=13$ |

giving $f(20)=13$. The sequence is:
$$1,\,2,\,2,\,3,\,4,\,4,\,5,\,5,\,6,\,7,\,7,\,8,\,9,\,9,\,10,\,10,\,11,\,12,\,12,\,13.$$

**Verification of consistency.** Every value $f(n)$ for $n\le 20$ satisfies $f(n)\le n$, and each $f(f(n))$ references an index $\le n$ whose value was already fixed — so the recurrence is self-consistent with no contradictions. The function extends to all of $\mathbb{N}$ by the same recurrence (the pattern of repeated values follows a Beatty/Wythoff sequence governed by the golden ratio, ensuring $f(n)\le n$ persists for all $n$).

**Computing the sum.**
$$\sum_{n=1}^{20}f(n)=1+2+2+3+4+4+5+5+6+7+7+8+9+9+10+10+11+12+12+13.$$

Grouping: $1+(2+2)+3+(4+4)+(5+5)+6+(7+7)+8+(9+9)+(10+10)+11+(12+12)+13$
$$=1+4+3+8+10+6+14+8+18+20+11+24+13=140.$$

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{20}f(n)=140}$$

